msgid "Extract $P_1$ at each angle and compare to $\\sin^2(\\theta/2)$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-"
"mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/b1da86f2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On the curve you measured, $P_1$ first reaches its maximum at the $\\pi$ "
"pulse ($\\theta = \\pi$). Now suppose a device is miscalibrated, so that a "
"*nominal* angle $\\theta$ on the x-axis actually produces a rotation of "
"$1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(a)** At what nominal angle would the first maximum of $P_1$ now appear?\n"
"**(b)** Would applying a single $\\pi$ pulse be a sensitive way to detect this 5% error?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(a)** The maximum appears where the *actual* rotation equals $\\pi$, that "
"is, at the nominal angle $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; a "
"small leftward shift of the peak.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(b)** No. A nominal $\\pi$ pulse produces an actual rotation of "
"$1.05\\pi$, giving $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ instead of $1$"
" &mdash; a change too small to see against measurement noise. This is why, "
"to *calibrate* precisely, NMR spectroscopists amplify the error by applying "
"the rotation many times (for example the $360^\\circ$ method, or a long "
"pulse train), so a small per-pulse error accumulates into a large, "
"measurable deviation. We return to this idea in the challenge problems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Experiment: $T_1$ (spin-lattice relaxation) {#experiment-t-1-spin-lattice-"
"relaxation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Theory {#theory-2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Left alone, an excited spin gives up energy to its surroundings (the "
"\"lattice\") and relaxes back toward thermal equilibrium. The excited-state "
"population decays exponentially with the **spin-lattice relaxation time** "
"$T_1$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$P_1(\\tau) = e^{-\\tau/T_1}.$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For a chemist, $T_1$ sets the **recycle delay** you must wait between scans "
"for quantitative NMR, and it reports on **molecular tumbling** and dynamics."
" On a qubit it is the energy-relaxation time, and is one of the two main "
"numbers used to model the decoherence effects in a quantum computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The experiment.** Prepare $|1\\rangle$ with an $X$ gate (a $\\pi$ pulse), "
"wait a variable delay $\\tau$, then measure. The fraction still in "
"$|1\\rangle$ traces out the decay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A Bloch-sphere view {#a-bloch-sphere-view-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$T_1$ relaxation returns the excited state to equilibrium, so the Bloch "
"vector slides from $|1\\rangle$ back up to $|0\\rangle$ along the "
"$z$-axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<IBMVideo id=\"135063679\" title=\"Bloch-sphere animation of T1 relaxation "
"from the excited state toward equilibrium.\"/>\n"
msgstr ""

