#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Analyze: Fit and compare the two decays {#analyze-fit-and-compare-the-two-"
"decays}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-"
"mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/5f0f734c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we see both the Ramsey oscillation and the Hahn echo signal decay, as "
"expected. The associated time scales for each of these decays are $T_2^*$ "
"and $T_2$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You should typically find $T_2 \\gtrsim T_2^{*}$. Explain why in one "
"sentence, and say what physical effect the Hahn echo's $\\pi$ pulse removes,"
" as well as what it cannot remove.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$T_2^{*}$ includes both irreversible dephasing *and* reversible static "
"spread, so it decays faster; the echo's $\\pi$ pulse refocuses the **static,"
" reversible** dephasing (field inhomogeneity / fixed-frequency offsets), "
"leaving only the **irreversible** spin-spin dephasing, which is the true "
"$T_2$. The echo cannot undo fluctuations that change between the two halves "
"of the sequence.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"<span id=\"nmr-spectroscopy\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"NMR spectroscopy of a molecule: Chemical shift and $J$-coupling {#nmr-"
"spectroscopy-of-a-molecule-chemical-shift-and-j-coupling}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From one spin to a molecule {#from-one-spin-to-a-molecule}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The experiments so far characterized a single, isolated spin. Real chemical "
"NMR reads a *molecule* (many coupled spins at once) and uses the resulting "
"spectrum to infer features of the molecule's structure. This can tell us "
"about two effects:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Chemical shift.** Each chemically distinct nucleus precesses at a slightly"
" different frequency $\\nu_i$, because its local electrons shield the "
"applied field by different amounts. Peak *positions* therefore identify "
"chemical environments (a CH$_3$ proton versus an aromatic one, for "
"example).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Scalar $J$-coupling.** A through-bond interaction between two spins splits"
" each peak into a multiplet. A nucleus with $n$ *equivalent* neighbors is "
"split into $n+1$ lines with binomial intensities, the **$n+1$ rule**. The "
"splitting *pattern* reveals connectivity (which nuclei are bonded), and the "
"spacing is the coupling constant $J$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The experiment {#the-experiment}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To model this in our quantum computer, we need to implement a spin "
"Hamiltonian on the qubits. In the weak-coupling, high-field limit (chemical-"
"shift differences $\\gg$ couplings), the $XX$ and $YY$ parts of the coupling"
" average out, leaving only $ZZ$. So the Hamiltonian is (angular-frequency "
"units, $\\hbar=1$):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$H = \\underbrace{\\sum_i \\pi\\,\\nu_i\\, Z_i}_{\\text{chemical shift}} "
"\\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\sum_{i<j} \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\,J_{ij}\\, "
"Z_iZ_j}_{J\\text{-coupling}}.$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each nucleus is **one qubit**: the chemical shift is a single-qubit $Z$ "
"rotation, and the coupling is a two-qubit $ZZ$ interaction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The experiment proceeds as follows. A $\\pi/2$ pulse tips every spin into "
"the transverse plane, and the spins precess and couple for time $t$. We "
"record the complex transverse magnetization $s(t) = \\langle X\\rangle + "
"i\\langle Y\\rangle$ summed over all spins. As in the Ramsey experiment "
"above, this transverse magnetization traces out a decaying sinusoid as a "
"function of the precession time $t$. When we take the Fourier transform of "
"the signal, we get a spectrum with peaks at the chemical shift $\\nu_i$ "
"split by the scalar coupling $J_{ij}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"We simulate the molecule's evolution by mapping the time $t$ onto *rotation angles*: a larger $t$ means larger $Z$ and $ZZ$ rotations, not a longer or deeper circuit. So while the simulated state does advance with $t$ (that's what makes the free induction decay (FID) oscillate), the physical qubits never idle any longer and there's no $t$-dependent decoherence. The simulated FID therefore never decays on its own. For an infinitely long ideal FID the spectral lines would be infinitely sharp; our finite sampling window instead gives them finite resolution and can introduce spectral leakage. Real NMR lines have a finite width set by $T_2^*$. We add it back by multiplying the FID by a window $e^{-t/T_2^{*}}$ before the Fourier transform. This is called a **line-broadening apodization**, which spectroscopists apply in processing to set the linewidth (trading resolution for signal-to-noise). (An optional cell in the Appendix of this module shows a **Trotterized** alternative, where the decay instead comes from real decoherence that grows with circuit depth.)\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The AX system {#the-ax-system}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's start with the so-called AX system, a molecule consisting of two "
"coupled, chemically distinct spins. The labels A and X denote the two "
"chemically distinct spin environments. The letters are arbitrary, but the "
"fact that they are far apart in the alphabet illustrates their chemical "
"dissimilarity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map and optimize {#map-and-optimize}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We write one function that, given the chemical-shift offsets $\\nu_i$ and "
"couplings $J_{ij}$, returns a **parameterized** state-preparation circuit. "
"The $\\pi/2$ pulse tips the spins to the equator, then the $Z$ rotations "
"simulate the evolution of the state under the chemical-shift and "
"$J$-coupling terms. Each $\\nu_i$ is a small precession *offset* in Hz "
"relative to a chosen reference, or rotating-frame carrier. Here we set it "
"manually; in a real molecule, electron shielding changes the local magnetic "
"field and shifts each nucleus's resonance relative to that reference. This "
"is modeled by the single-qubit $R_Z$ gates, one per spin, and each $\\nu_i$ "
"fixes where that spin's peak sits in the spectrum. The two-qubit $ZZ$ gate "
"applies the coupling $J_{ij}$, which splits each peak into a multiplet and "
"sets the spacing between its lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Execute and post-process {#execute-and-post-process}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We evaluate the circuit over a grid of evolution times, and the "
"**Estimator** returns $\\langle X\\rangle$ and $\\langle Y\\rangle$ at each."
" We combine these into the complex FID $\\langle X\\rangle + i\\langle "
"Y\\rangle$, apply the apodization window, and Fourier transform the result "
"to obtain the spectrum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-"
"mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/26bcc4bc-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Extension: An AX$_2$ system and the $n+1$ rule {#extension-an-ax-2-system-"
"and-the-n1-rule}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now add a second equivalent neighbor. In an **AX$_2$** system one **A** spin"
" couples, with the same $J$, to two **equivalent X** spins. (Remember, X is "
"just a label for the two equivalent spins; it has nothing to do with the "
"Pauli $X$ operators. Every coupling below is still $ZZ$.) Spin A then sees "
"four neighbor configurations $(\\uparrow\\uparrow,\\ \\uparrow\\downarrow,\\"
" \\downarrow\\uparrow,\\ \\downarrow\\downarrow)$; the two mixed ones "
"coincide, so A is split into a **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2 \\Rightarrow n+1 = 3$"
" lines). Each X spin sees only A and stays a doublet. Equivalent spins do "
"not split each other, so we include no X–X coupling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A real molecule with this pattern is **1,1,2-trichloroethane** "
"(Cl$_2$CH–CH$_2$Cl). When targeting the hydrogen spins (which is the most "
"common form of NMR), the single hydrogen proton in **CH** plays the role of "
"**A**, and the two equivalent **CH$_2$** protons are the two **X** spins. "
"Now just count neighbors: the CH proton has *two* neighbors (the CH$_2$ "
"pair), so its signal splits into a **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2$); each CH$_2$ "
"proton has only *one* neighbor (the CH), so the CH$_2$ signal is a **1:1 "
"doublet** ($n=1$). Note that the group with *fewer* protons (CH) shows "
"*more* lines; the line count reflects how many neighbors a signal has, not "
"how many protons share it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![1,1,2-trichloroethane: the CH proton is A, the two equivalent CH$_2$ "
"protons are X](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/nmr-with-"
"qiskit/trichloroethane_ax.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Below, we simulate an AX$_2$ system with three qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read each question, think about your answer, then click the triangle to "
"reveal the solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the AX spectrum the two doublets are separated (center-to-center) by 12 "
"Hz, and the two lines within each doublet are 2 Hz apart. Which number is "
"the chemical-shift difference and which is the coupling constant $J$? If you"
" moved to a spectrometer with twice the magnetic field, which one would "
"change?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The 12 Hz center-to-center separation is the **chemical-shift difference** "
"($\\nu_1-\\nu_2$); the 2 Hz line spacing is the **coupling constant $J$**. "
"Chemical shifts come from shielding of $B_0$, so they scale with the field. "
"At twice the field the separation doubles to 24 Hz. The coupling $J$ is a "
"through-bond molecular property and is field-independent, so it stays 2 Hz. "
"Chemists tell shift from coupling by watching how a splitting scales with "
"field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Using the $n+1$ rule, predict the A multiplet if a *third* equivalent X spin"
" were added (an AX$_3$ system), and explain why the two equivalent X spins "
"in AX$_2$ produce no splitting of each other.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
"With $n=3$ equivalent neighbors, A becomes a 1:3:3:1 quartet. Equivalent spins don't split each other because their coupling shifts the relevant energy levels all by the same amount, so the gaps between those levels &mdash; the frequencies we see &mdash; don't change.\n"
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Conclusion {#conclusion}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We have used a real quantum computer to explore how nuclear spins precess "
"and decohere in an NMR experiment, and how to interpret the resulting "
"spectra. It's worth stepping back to review why this was possible at all, "
"and why it's more than a curiosity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A qubit and a spin-1/2 nucleus are, mathematically, the same object: two-"
"level quantum systems living on the Bloch sphere, rotated by resonant "
"pulses, precessing about an axis, and losing coherence to their "
"surroundings. The entire vocabulary of this module (the Larmor frequency, "
"the rotating frame, $T_1$, $T_2$, and $T_2^*$, Ramsey fringes, the Hahn "
"echo) belongs to NMR and quantum computing equally. What a chemist calls a "
"*chemical shift*, a quantum engineer calls a *single-qubit $Z$ rotation*; "
"what a chemist calls a *$J$-coupling*, a quantum engineer calls a *$ZZ$ "
"interaction*. The two fields have been describing the same physics in "
"different words.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That connection is historical, not just conceptual: NMR was one of the first"
" platforms to run real quantum algorithms. By 2001, a molecule in solution "
"had executed Shor's algorithm to factor 15, with each nuclear spin serving "
"as a qubit. NMR also handed quantum computing much of its control toolkit. "
"The spin echo, CPMG pulse trains, composite pulses, and dynamical decoupling"
" were all invented by spectroscopists decades before qubits needed them to "
"fight decoherence. There is a satisfying symmetry in what we just did: the "
"field that gave birth to early quantum computers is now something a quantum "
"computer can simulate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Review and questions {#review-and-questions}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Critical concepts {#critical-concepts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A qubit is an engineered two-level system equivalent to a spin-1/2; its "
"$|0\\rangle/|1\\rangle$, frequency, gates, $T_1$, and $T_2$ are the direct "
"analogs of NMR quantities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A resonant pulse rotates the Bloch vector; the flip angle sets the rotation,"
" and the Rabi/nutation curve $P_1=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)$ identifies $\\pi$ and "
"$\\pi/2$ rotations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$T_1$ is energy (spin-lattice) relaxation; $T_2$ is phase (spin-spin) "
"relaxation; $T_2^{*}$ additionally includes reversible static dephasing that"
" a Hahn echo refocuses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A molecule is a set of coupled spins: chemical shift sets each peak's "
"position, and $J$-coupling splits peaks into multiplets following the $n+1$ "
"rule, which together encode chemical environment and connectivity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "True / false {#true--false}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F The Larmor frequency is the energy gap between the two spin states "
"divided by $\\hbar$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T/F A $90^\\circ$ ($\\pi/2$) pulse fully inverts an equilibrium spin.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T/F $T_2^{*}$ is generally longer than $T_2$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T/F The Hahn echo refocuses irreversible spin-spin dephasing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T/F In an AX$_2$ system the A nucleus appears as a 1:2:1 triplet.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F The coupling constant $J$ doubles when you double the spectrometer's "
"magnetic field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '7. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F Removing Qiskit Pulse makes it impossible to measure $T_1$ on current "
"hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Short answer {#short-answer}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Explain in two sentences why a single $\\pi$ pulse is a poor way to detect a"
" $5\\%$ flip-angle error, and how chaining pulses fixes this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A sample shows broad NMR lines. In terms of $T_2$, what does that tell you, "
"and what qubit quantity is the analog?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Give the qubit analog of each: (a) the recycle delay, (b) the $90^\\circ$ "
"pulse, (c) the chemical shift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Using the $n+1$ rule, explain why an ethyl group (CH$_3$CH$_2$–) shows a "
"1:2:1 triplet and a 1:3:3:1 quartet, and identify which group produces which"
" multiplet.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Challenge problems {#challenge-problems}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Carr-Purcell train.** Modify the Hahn echo to insert $n$ equally spaced "
"$\\pi$ pulses (a CPMG-style train) and measure $T_2$ as a function of $n$. "
"Discuss why more refocusing pulses can lengthen the apparent $T_2$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Pulse-error amplification.** Build a circuit that applies $R_X(\\pi)$ "
"$\\,n$ times to $|0\\rangle$ and plot $P_1$ vs $n$ for a deliberately mis-"
"set angle $R_X(1.05\\pi)$. Show how the error becomes visible as $n$ grows. "
"**Hint:** put a barrier after every rotation; otherwise the transpiler may "
"combine the repeated rotations into a single equivalent gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Ethyl group.** Using `fid_circuit`, build the proton spectrum of an ethyl "
"fragment (CH$_3$–CH$_2$): five qubits, with the three CH$_3$ protons sharing"
" one chemical shift and the two CH$_2$ protons another, and every "
"CH$_3$–CH$_2$ pair coupled by the same $J$ (no coupling within an equivalent"
" group). Predict the pattern with the $n+1$ rule, then simulate it; you "
"should recover a 1:3:3:1 quartet (CH$_2$) and a 1:2:1 triplet (CH$_3$).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Appendix (optional): Trotterized evolution — let decoherence set the "
"linewidth {#appendix-optional-trotterized-evolution-let-decoherence-set-the-"
"linewidth}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the [NMR spectroscopy of a molecule](#nmr-spectroscopy) section, the "
"evolution $e^{-iHt}$ is applied in a single layer whose depth does **not** "
"grow with $t$, so the FID does not decay on its own and we impose the "
"linewidth with the $e^{-t/T_2}$ apodization window. This optional cell takes"
" the opposite approach. It breaks the evolution into $p$ **Trotter steps** "
"and puts a **barrier** after each step (so the transpiler cannot merge them "
"back into one layer). Time point $k$ uses $k$ steps, so the circuit depth "
"grows with time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because every term in $H$ commutes, this is *exact*: on a noiseless "
"simulator it gives the same sharp peaks. But on a **noise-model backend or "
"real hardware** the accumulating gate error makes the FID decay by itself, "
"and that decay is what sets the linewidth. This is the "
"$T_2\\leftrightarrow$linewidth idea of the [$T_2$, $T_2^*$ "
"experiment](#t2t2star) section, now arising from real decoherence instead of"
" a processing window. We run both the **AX** system (two doublets) and the "
"**AX$_2$** system (a 1:2:1 triplet plus a doublet) this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two practical notes to consider. First, the cost is depth: the deepest AX "
"circuit uses $\\sim\\!47$ two-qubit gates and the AX$_2$ version about twice"
" that, so both are far noisier and more expensive than the exact version. "
"Secondly, each spin system is still submitted as one job, and every time "
"point reuses the same physical-qubit layout selected in the spectroscopy "
"section. If `USE_BATCH=True`, these jobs also use the optional notebook-wide"
" Batch.\n"
msgstr ""
